tmux crashes (killing the whole server and every shell in it) when the
copy-mode refresh-from-pane command — bound to "r" in both default
copy-mode key tables — replaces the copy-mode backing screen with a
clone that has less history than the current scroll offset. The pane
can legitimately have less history than before, most commonly because
it switched to the alternate screen (hsize 0) after copy mode was
entered on the normal screen and scrolled up. window_copy_size_changed
→ window_copy_clear_selection then computes
py = screen_hsize(backing) + cy - oy with the stale larger oy, the
u_int subtraction wraps to a huge line number, and grid_line_length
reads far outside the grid (observed as SIGBUS in unallocated reserved
VM). Upstream later restructured this path and clamps oy the same way;
3.6a does not. The grid_line_length bounds guard additionally turns any
other out-of-range line-length lookup into a harmless empty-line result
instead of a server-killing wild read.

--- a/window-copy.c
+++ b/window-copy.c
@@ -2682,6 +2682,9 @@
 	data->backing = window_copy_clone_screen(&wp->base, &data->screen, NULL,
 	    NULL, wme->swp != wme->wp);

+	if (data->oy > screen_hsize(data->backing))
+		data->oy = screen_hsize(data->backing);
+
 	window_copy_size_changed(wme);
 	return (WINDOW_COPY_CMD_REDRAW);
 }
--- a/grid.c
+++ b/grid.c
@@ -1549,6 +1549,8 @@
 	struct grid_cell	gc;
 	u_int			px;

+	if (py >= gd->hsize + gd->sy)
+		return (0);
 	px = grid_get_line(gd, py)->cellsize;
 	if (px > gd->sx)
 		px = gd->sx;
